337 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 163 0 R
/T (Other historically accepted methods, such as bite-mark comparison and microscopic hair analysis, have recently been severely discredited by scientific reviews, leading to the exoneration of dozens of wrongfully convicted individuals who spent decades behind bars.)
/E (Other historically accepted methods, such as bite-mark comparison and microscopic hair analysis, have recently been severely discredited by scientific reviews, leading to the exoneration of dozens of wrongfully convicted individuals who spent decades behind bars.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
338 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 163 0 R
/T (Furthermore, as we crash into the era of deepfakes and AI-generated realities, digital forensics faces an unprecedented crisis. When a video, an audio recording, or a digital photograph can be perfectly fabricated to frame an innocent party, what truly constitutes "hard evidence" anymore? The justice system is struggling to keep pace with technology that can seamlessly manipulate reality.)
/E (Furthermore, as we crash into the era of deepfakes and AI-generated realities, digital forensics faces an unprecedented crisis. When a video, an audio recording, or a digital photograph can be perfectly fabricated to frame an innocent party, what truly constitutes "hard evidence" anymore? The justice system is struggling to keep pace with technology that can seamlessly manipulate reality.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
339 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 163 0 R
/T (Ultimately, forensic science is a powerful tool, not a crystal ball. It is an evolving, imperfect discipline governed by human beings, and humans inevitably make mistakes. For the modern legal system to be truly just, we must strip away the illusion of forensic perfection. We must constantly question the science, rigorously challenge the methods in court, and remember that in the pursuit of justice, blind faith is the greatest enemy of truth. )
/E (Ultimately, forensic science is a powerful tool, not a crystal ball. It is an evolving, imperfect discipline governed by human beings, and humans inevitably make mistakes. For the modern legal system to be truly just, we must strip away the illusion of forensic perfection. We must constantly question the science, rigorously challenge the methods in court, and remember that in the pursuit of justice, blind faith is the greatest enemy of truth. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [5]
