443 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (Thomas Alva Edison was born on February 11, 1847, in the American village of Milan. When he was 10 years old, he set up his own laboratory in the basement of his house. In 1860, he worked in the telegraph office in the United States and Canada.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
444 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (In 1876, he sold his telegraph printing machine. With the money he earned, he set up his own laboratory in Menye Park, New York, where he developed the carbon resistance transmitter and the gramophone , the world's first machine that could record and reproduce the human voice.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
445 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 174 0 R
/E (Let's learn about Thomas Alva Edison \(1847-1931\),  )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
446 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 175 0 R
/Pg 37 0 R
/A [794 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
447 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (In 1879, he made another great invention. It was the electric bulb that we all use today. In 1880, he lit up Moklo Park for the first time with 100 electric bulbs. This was just one of his discoveries. The first city in the world to be illuminated by electric bulbs was New York City .)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
448 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (That glorious day is recorded as September 4, 1882. That night, 75 kilometers of cables were laid, providing electricity to over 1,000 homes in the area for the first time.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
449 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (In 1887, he built a new science laboratory in West Orange County. In this laboratory, he developed the first motion picture camera for still photography, the Kinetograph. This camera used Eastman film. After cleaning it, people came to see it in amazement. People considered Edison a technological genius.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
450 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (In 1889, a great international exhibition was held in Paris. The most important event was the exhibition of a huge number of new scientific discoveries, all of which were the work of one man. This great genius, Thomas Alva Edison, was warmly welcomed in Paris.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
451 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 175 0 R
/E (He had patented over 100 inventions that claimed his inventions and rights. His determination to do so may have been influenced by his childhood. His mother, a teacher, provided him with everything he needed. As a child, he hoped to earn his own money for his research. Accordingly, he sold newspapers and sweets to passengers on the Grand Trunk Railway between Port Peoria and Detroit. In 1861, he was eager to learn about the American Civil War, so he bought an old hand-operated printing press for $12. He set up his own printing press on the train, using borrowed paper.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
