457 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (He named it the Grand Trunks Herald. It was the first newspaper to be printed on a train. He also set up a small laboratory on the train, where he conducted various experiments and recorded them in his spare time.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (An accident while selling newspapers left him deaf in one ear. Moreover, his laboratory on the train caught fire, so the angry officers ordered him not to trade from the train. They also threw out the laboratory supplies. When he tried to sell the telegraph machine he had built through obstacles to the head of the stock exchange at the time for $3,000, the head realized its value and gave him $40,000. Edison's good times began from then on. However, he continued to engage in scientific research throughout his life. Despite his great discoveries, he was a very introspective and modest person. He often said that although 1% of this intellectual power of mine was a divine inheritance, the remaining 99% was his own sweat and tears. This great man breathed his last on October 31, 1931. In honor of him, Thomas Alva Edison's name was added to the Book of Greatest Americans in 1960.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (-This article is an excerpt from the book The Greatest of the World by K.R. Aryasinghe-)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [5]
>>
